WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.956
Threat modeling an MCP system means separating the instruction path, the data path,

00:00:04.956 --> 00:00:06.450
and the action path.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:10.185
The instruction path includes user prompts, system instructions, tool descriptions,

00:00:10.185 --> 00:00:13.920
prompt templates, and external content that may influence model behavior.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.029
The data path includes resources, files, retrieval results, tool responses, logs,

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:19.149
and downstream records.

00:00:19.149 --> 00:00:24.005
The action path includes tool calls that can read, write, send, modify, approve,

00:00:24.005 --> 00:00:26.246
or trigger activity in other systems.

00:00:26.246 --> 00:00:30.729
Prompt injection is a high-level risk where untrusted instructions influence model behavior

00:00:30.729 --> 00:00:33.717
in a way the system did not intend.

00:00:33.717 --> 00:00:39.320
Indirect prompt injection is especially relevant because the model may read external content from documents,

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.055
tickets, web pages, emails, repositories, or resources exposed through MCP.

00:00:43.055 --> 00:00:46.043
The training point is not to memorize payloads.

00:00:46.043 --> 00:00:52.019
The stable lesson is that external content should be treated as data with provenance and policy,

00:00:52.019 --> 00:00:55.381
not as trusted instructions that can override application rules.

00:00:55.381 --> 00:00:57.622
Tool metadata can also create risk.

00:00:57.622 --> 00:01:02.104
A malicious or misleading tool description may shape when the model chooses

00:01:02.104 --> 00:01:04.719
a tool or what arguments it sends.

00:01:04.719 --> 00:01:08.454
Overbroad permissions can turn a narrow task into broad access.

00:01:08.454 --> 00:01:12.936
Confused deputy risk appears when a component with legitimate authority is tricked

00:01:12.936 --> 00:01:15.924
into using that authority for an unintended purpose.

00:01:15.924 --> 00:01:18.913
Supply-chain risk appears when a server, dependency, package,

00:01:18.913 --> 00:01:21.901
or installation path is trusted without enough review.

00:01:21.901 --> 00:01:27.130
A practical MCP threat model asks three blunt questions: what can the agent read,

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:30.492
what can it call, and what can it change?

00:01:30.492 --> 00:01:35.721
Then it tests the answers against misuse scenarios such as data leakage, unauthorized actions,

00:01:35.721 --> 00:01:39.456
stale or untrusted resources, malicious server registration, over-trusting model output,

00:01:39.456 --> 00:01:40.577
and approval bypass.

00:01:40.577 --> 00:01:44.685
The resulting controls should live outside the model: authorization checks, allowlists,

00:01:44.685 --> 00:01:47.300
validation, approvals, isolation, logging, and incident response.
